{
  "teamId": 144,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 Atlanta Braves arrived as defending World Series champions with a chip on their shoulder and a roster hungry to prove the previous October was no fluke. April was a stumble — a 10-12 record that raised quiet questions — but the offense showed flashes of what was coming, with Adam Duvall and Dansby Swanson delivering clutch extra-base hits to salvage games early in the month. The roster was still finding its footing, and the bullpen, anchored by new addition Kenley Jansen, was already showing the inconsistency that would become a recurring headache through the spring.\n\nMay brought the growing pains into full view. Swanson's three-run homer on the 13th provided a signature moment, but the very same night the Braves surrendered a brutal answer, and Jansen endured a pair of gut-punch outings — Bryce Harper's tenth homer off him on May 24 the most damaging of all. A 13-15 May record reflected a team still searching. But just beneath the surface, the pieces were assembling. Marcell Ozuna was finding his stroke, and the organization was about to unveil a secret weapon.\n\nJune changed everything. A 21-6 record — one of the best months in franchise history — announced the Braves as the class of the National League. Rookie center fielder Michael Harris II arrived in late May and immediately electrified the lineup, homering in July and playing defense that left jaws on the floor. Orlando Arcia, Adam Duvall, and Matt Olson delivered clutch hits through the summer, and the offense that had looked so dangerous on paper finally became dominant in practice. July and August brought more of the same — winning records, timely hitting from Riley and Olson, and even unexpected heroes like Robbie Grossman, whose two-run walk-off homer on September 11th was the single highest-leverage play of the entire season. The Braves rolled to 101 wins, the best record in the NL.\n\nOctober, however, belonged to the Philadelphia Phillies. In the NLDS, Atlanta ran into a Phillies club riding the kind of furious momentum that makes October baseball so unforgiving. Bryce Harper and his teammates punished every Braves mistake — Jesse Chavez surrendered a crushing multi-run single in Game 1, Spencer Strider gave up a three-run homer in Game 3, and Charlie Morton couldn't hold the lead in Game 4. The Braves scratched out moments — Travis d'Arnaud homered, Austin Riley delivered a clutch single — but it wasn't enough. A 101-win season ended not with a championship parade but with the familiar sting of an early exit, a reminder that in baseball, the best team in April through September doesn't always get the last word.",
  "chapters": [
